---
title: Quick Start
description: Deploy your first Pika chat application in 15 minutes
---

Get up and running with Pika Platform in 15 minutes. This guide takes you from zero to your first working chat application as quickly as possible.

## Prerequisites

Before you begin, verify you have these installed:

```bash
# Check Node.js version (need 22+)
node --version

# Check if pnpm is installed
pnpm --version

# Check if Git is installed
git --version
```

{% aside type="note" title="Need to Install?" %}
If any of these commands fail, follow the [detailed installation guide](/getting-started/installation/) to set up your system.

**Quick Install Commands:**
- **pnpm**: `npm install -g pnpm` or `curl -fsSL https://get.pnpm.io/install.sh | sh -`
- **Node.js**: Download from [nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/) or use your package manager
{% /aside %}

## Step 1: Install Pika CLI

Install the Pika command-line tool globally:

```bash
pnpm install -g pika-app
```

Verify the installation:

```bash
pika --version
```

## Step 2: Create Your Application

Create a new Pika application with a single command:

```bash
pika create-app my-chat-app
```

This command will:
- Clone the Pika framework repository
- Set up the complete project structure
- Install all dependencies
- Configure the project for your use case

{% aside type="tip" title="What Gets Created" %}
This creates a **generic framework** for defining multiple chat applications and agents. You typically only need **one Pika installation per AWS account**. The framework provides the infrastructure to host and manage multiple chat apps.
{% /aside %}

The installation process takes 2-5 minutes depending on your internet connection.

## Step 3: Navigate to Your Project

```bash
cd my-chat-app
```

Take a moment to explore the structure:

```
my-chat-app/
├── apps/
│   ├── pika-chat/          # Main chat frontend
│   └── samples/            # Sample applications
├── services/
│   ├── pika/              # Core backend service
│   └── samples/           # Sample services (like weather)
├── packages/              # Shared packages
└── pika-config.ts         # Project configuration
```

## Step 4: Configure Your Project

Update the project names in `pika-config.ts` to customize your installation:

```typescript
export const pikaConfig: PikaConfig = {
    pika: {
        projNameL: 'mycompany',
        projNameKebabCase: 'mycompany',
        projNameTitleCase: 'MyCompany',
        projNameCamel: 'mycompany',
        projNameHuman: 'My Company'
    },
    pikaChat: {
        projNameL: 'mycompanychat',
        projNameKebabCase: 'mycompany-chat',
        projNameTitleCase: 'MyCompanyChat',
        projNameCamel: 'myCompanyChat',
        projNameHuman: 'My Company Chat'
    }
};
```

{% aside type="note" title="Why Configure Names?" %}
These names are used for AWS stack and resource names. Customizing them helps avoid conflicts with other projects and makes your resources easier to identify in the AWS console.
{% /aside %}

## Step 5: Start Development Mode

Start the development server:

```bash
pnpm dev
```

This command:
- Builds the project
- Starts the frontend development server
- Opens your browser to `http://localhost:3000`

{% aside type="tip" title="First Run Takes Longer" %}
The first build can take 2-3 minutes as TypeScript compiles all packages. Subsequent starts are much faster thanks to caching.
{% /aside %}

## Step 6: Explore Your Application

Your application is now running at `http://localhost:3000`!

### What You Can Do:

{% card title="Main Chat Interface" icon="star" %}
The home page shows available chat applications. With the default setup, you'll see the Weather chat app.
{% /card %}

{% card title="Weather Chat App" icon="approve-check" %}
Visit `http://localhost:3000/chat/weather` to interact with the sample weather agent. Try asking "What's the weather in Seattle?"
{% /card %}

{% card title="Sample Enterprise Site" icon="rocket" %}
The sample at `http://localhost:5173` demonstrates how to embed Pika chat into an existing website using an iframe.
{% /card %}

## Understanding the Default Setup

### Mock Authentication

{% aside type="caution" title="Development Only" %}
By default, Pika uses a mock authentication provider that automatically logs you in as a test user. This is **for development only** and provides no real security. Before deploying to production, you must implement a custom authentication provider.
{% /aside %}

The mock setup:
- Hardcodes a single test user
- No password required
- Automatically authenticates anyone who visits

See the [Authentication Guide](/how-to/authentication/) to implement real authentication.

### Sample Weather Service

The weather service demonstrates:
- How to define a chat app
- How to create an agent with tools
- How agents use tools to answer questions
- How responses are rendered in the UI

This is your template for creating your own chat apps and agents.

## Next Steps

Now that you have Pika running, you have several options:

### Option 1: Build Your First Custom Agent

Follow the [Hello World Tutorial](/getting-started/hello-world/) to create your first custom agent from scratch.

### Option 2: Deploy to AWS

To test with real AWS services:
1. Configure AWS CLI: `aws configure`
2. Deploy backend: `cd services/pika && pnpm build && pnpm run cdk:deploy`
3. Deploy sample: `cd services/samples/weather && pnpm build && pnpm run cdk:deploy`

See [Local Development Guide](/how-to/local-development/) for details.

### Option 3: Learn More About Pika

- **[Why Pika?](/why/)** - Understand the platform's design philosophy
- **[Capabilities](/capabilities/)** - Explore what you can build
- **[Concepts](/concepts/)** - Learn how Pika works under the hood

### Option 4: Customize Your Installation

- **[Authentication](/how-to/authentication/)** - Implement real authentication
- **[Customization](/how-to/customization/)** - Customize the UI and behavior
- **[Access Control](/how-to/access-control/)** - Configure who can access what

## Common Quick Start Issues

### Port 3000 Already in Use

If port 3000 is already in use:

```bash
# Kill the process using port 3000
lsof -ti:3000 | xargs kill -9

# Or use a different port
pnpm run dev -- --port 3001
```

### Dependencies Fail to Install

If pnpm install fails:

```bash
# Clear pnpm cache and retry
pnpm store prune
pnpm install --force
```

### TypeScript Errors During Build

If you see TypeScript compilation errors:

```bash
# Clean and rebuild
pnpm clean
pnpm install
pnpm build
```

